# Overview
#
# A clause is a subject and its verb, such as `the tests pass`. A colon
# between two clauses packs two points into one sentence (`The build
# failed: the cache was stale`), and the reader must work out how the
# second point relates to the first. The fix ends the sentence at the
# colon or joins the clauses with `because` or `so`. The rule sits at
# Recommended, because it tells a clause by word classes, and Harper's
# part-of-speech tagger marks many nouns as verbs.
#
# Expression
#
# A label is a word or two that names what follows its colon, such as
# `Usage:` or `Note:`. The pattern matches two words, then a word that
# carries the colon and is not a label from `labels`, and then the
# opening of a clause.
#
# Harper splits `image:` into `image` and `:` with no whitespace between
# them. A space in a pattern is a whitespace token, so the label guard
# sits glued to the colon.
#
# The two leading wildcards require two words before the word that
# carries the colon. A clause has a subject and a verb before its colon,
# so the pattern takes three words as the shortest clause before a
# colon. `let scope "Sentence"` lets a match cross the colon and keeps
# the match inside one sentence. A label of one or two words at the
# start of its sentence then has too few words before its colon. The
# pattern cannot start there.
#
# After the colon, the pattern takes one of two openings. The first is
# `it`, `they`, `we`, `you`, `I`, `there`, `he`, or `she` and one more
# word. The second is a determiner or a pronoun, and then a sign of a
# clause within the next three words. A determiner is a word that
# introduces a noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`. The tagger marks
# each word with a tag for its word class, and the pattern names the two
# classes as `DET` (a determiner) and `PRON` (a pronoun).
#
# Three signs mark a clause. One is an auxiliary, a helping verb such as
# `is`, `has`, or `can` (`the cache is cold`). `aux` holds such verbs
# and the tag `AUX` (a helping verb), and an auxiliary counts as the
# first, second, or third of the three words. Another sign is an adverb
# from `adverbs`, and it counts as the second or third word (`the tests
# always pass`). An adverb is a word that describes a verb, an
# adjective, or a whole sentence, such as `safely` or `also`. The last
# sign is a verb, and it counts only as the third word (`the first rule
# wins`). The pattern writes it as `<VERB, !PROG>`, the tag `VERB` (a
# verb) without `PROG` (the `-ing` form of a verb). When the sign is the
# third word, the second word must not come from `rel`, a list that
# holds words such as `that` and `you`.
#
# False Positives
#
# Docs open lines with labels of every kind (`Usage:`, `Returns:`, `Step
# 1:`, `Known issue:`, a speaker name, a conventional-commit type). Each
# label is one or two words at the start of its sentence, so the two
# leading wildcards keep it out. `labels` still covers a label that
# follows other words.
#
# A verb does not count as the first or second word after the
# determiner, because the tagger marks many nouns and participles as
# verbs. A participle is the `-ing` or `-ed` form of a verb, such as
# `returning` or `cached`. Without that limit, `the path passed by
# Claude Code`, `the value set by the environment`, and `the section on
# caching` all read as clauses. An adverb does not count directly after
# a determiner such as `the` (`the only value`, `the always-on mode`). A
# pronoun such as `that` or `you` in the second position marks a noun
# phrase, a noun and the words attached to it, and not a clause (`the
# tag that was pushed`, `the host you are targeting`).
#
# As the third word, a noun that the tagger marks as a verb still draws
# a finding (`The flag sets one field: the retry count limit`).
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 207 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 18 of 20 sampled
# hits had a clause on each side of the colon. One misfire followed a
# label of three words (`Less rare case: We also get here`). In the
# other misfire, the words before the colon were not a full clause (`the
# answer is: it depends`).
expr adverbs [never, always, also, only, silently, still, then, already, just, now, usually, often, sometimes, rarely, immediately, eventually]
expr aux [AUX, is, are, was, were, has, have, had, does, do, did, will, would, can, could, should, must, may, might, shall]
expr labels [Note, Warning, Tip, Caution, Important, Deprecated, TODO, FIXME, BUG, HACK, XXX]
expr rel [that, which, who, whose, where, when, it, you, we, they, I, he, she]
expr main * * !@labels: [([it, they, we, you, I, there, he, she] *), ([DET, PRON] [@aux, (* [@aux, @adverbs]), (* !@rel [@aux, @adverbs, <VERB, !PROG>])])]
let message "Colon between two clauses. End the sentence with a period, or join the clauses with `because` or `so`."
let description "Flags a colon that follows a clause and introduces a subject pronoun, or a determiner and a verb within three words."
let becomes ""
let kind "Punctuation"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Note: the cache is cold on first run."
allows "Tip: you can skip the check."
allows "Run this command: make test"
allows "The options are: fast, slow, and medium."
allows "Default: the most recent tag."
allows "Deprecated: this method returns nil."
allows "TODO: it needs a lock."
allows "Usage: the program reads from stdin and writes to stdout."
allows "Returns: the value stored in the cache, or nil."
allows "Known issue: the cursor is hidden after exit."
allows "Step 1: you install the package."
allows "fix: the parser crashed on empty input."
allows "Alice: I think the cache is stale."
allows "Default: the only supported value."
allows "The hook has one input: the path that changed."
allows "The switch takes one argument: the host you are targeting."
allows "It ships two artifacts: a tarball built from source and a checksum."
